course: biology
lesson: 93
title: The aviary: the walk-through tropical house
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A walk-through aviary is a huge netted house where birds fly free over your head while you walk along the paths, and two sets of doors stop anyone flying out.
explain: Every bird there shows a different way to live: hummingbirds hover like tiny helicopters at sugar water, a toucan tosses a grape up and catches it, a kookaburra laughs from a branch.
explain: Colours and beaks have reasons: a scarlet ibis is red because of the red pigments in its crabs and shrimps, and a toucan's giant beak is light and full of air spaces.
explain: Keepers watch closely, because birds hide illness; a bird weighed in a cup on a scale can tell the keeper it is unwell long before it looks ill.
explain: In The Lion King, Zazu is a red-billed hornbill; real hornbills do not talk or serve kings, but the beak and the African savanna are true to life.
fact: Walk-through aviaries let birds fly freely, which keeps them healthier and more natural, and double doors stop a bird slipping out when someone enters.
fact: Scarlet ibises get their red colour from pigments in the crabs and shrimps they eat, so zoos add the pigment to their food to keep them red; without it, new feathers grow in paler.
fact: Hummingbirds beat their wings up to about 80 times a second, can fly backwards, drink nectar many times an hour because hovering burns so much energy, and save energy at night in a deep sleep called torpor.
fact: A hornbill's huge beak is mostly hollow and light, and a female hornbill seals herself into a tree hole with mud while the male feeds her through a slit.
fact: Many pheasants come from Asia; the males are colourful to impress, while the brown females sit on the nest and need to hide.
fact: The common pheasant in British fields was brought from Asia long ago for hunting.
fact: Swans, many parrots and albatrosses keep the same partner for years, and some albatross pairs stay together for 50 years or more.
fact: Many birds that eat seeds feed their chicks insects for protein, and a pair of blue tits can bring around 10 000 caterpillars to one brood; most birds nest in spring, when insects are most plentiful.
fact: The Australian kookaburra's call sounds like loud laughter, and families call together at dawn and dusk to say «this is our land».
fact: Keepers weigh birds often, because sick birds hide illness, since in the wild a weak-looking bird is more likely to be caught, and losing weight is often the first sign.
fact: Zoos ask visitors not to pick up feathers, for hygiene and because feathers of some protected birds may not be kept without a permit; birds replace feathers in a moult.
fact: A toucan throws food up and catches it in its beak tip, and the beak also lets out heat like a radiator to help the bird cool down.
fact: Parrots and toucans have two toes pointing forward and two pointing back, while most perching birds have three forward and one back.
exercise: Why does a walk-through aviary have two sets of doors? | So a bird cannot slip out when someone enters | So visitors can leave faster | So two families can come in | To keep the rain out
exercise: Why are birds kept in big walk-through aviaries? | They are healthier and act more naturally when they can fly | Big aviaries are much cheaper to run than small cages | Birds cannot live in small spaces at all | The birds do not like people
exercise: Where does a scarlet ibis get its red colour? | From pigments in the crabs and shrimps it eats | From the hot tropical sun | From red mud in the pond | It is born bright red
exercise: How many times a second can a hummingbird beat its wings? | Up to about 80 | About 2 or 3 times | About 1,000 times | Only about 10 times
exercise: Which way can a hummingbird fly that most birds cannot? | Backwards | Upside down for hours | Underwater | Only in circles
exercise: What is a hornbill's huge beak like inside? | Mostly hollow and light | Solid and heavy like stone | Full of water | Full of teeth
exercise: What does a female hornbill do while she sits on her eggs? | Seals herself into a tree hole with mud | Builds a grassy nest on the ground | Leaves the eggs to the male | Carries the eggs in her beak
exercise: Why is a female pheasant brown? | She sits on the nest and needs to hide | Brown feathers keep out rain | She is always too young to grow bright feathers | Her colours fade in the sun
exercise: Where did the common pheasant of British fields first come from? | Asia | Australia | South America | Antarctica
exercise: Which birds can keep the same partner for 50 years or more? | Some albatrosses | All kinds of hummingbirds | Only the kookaburras | Pheasants in the woods
exercise: Why does the keeper put live mealworms out in the aviary? | Chicks need insect protein to grow | The birds weave them into nests | Mealworms clean the aviary floor | Birds play with them as toys
exercise: What is a kookaburra saying with its loud laughing call? | This is our land | I am happy | Danger, run away | Please feed me
exercise: Why do keepers weigh small birds often? | Weight loss is often the first sign of illness | To find the strongest bird | To check the birds are not too heavy to fly | To count how many birds there are
exercise: Why should you not pick up feathers in the aviary? | Hygiene, and some feathers are protected by law | Feathers grow back into birds | The keepers count every feather each night | The birds need them for their nests
exercise: What is the name for when a bird replaces its feathers? | A moult | A migration | A hibernation | A brood of chicks
exercise: How does a toucan eat a grape? | It tosses it up and catches it in its beak tip | It squashes it on a branch | It holds the grape in one foot and nibbles it slowly | It swallows it straight from the vine
exercise: What else does a toucan's big beak do? | Lets out heat to cool the bird down | Stores water inside it for the long dry days | Makes the bird float in water | Keeps its eggs warm
test: 4 hummingbirds at the feeder, 2 more zip in. how many wings flappin | 12 | 6 | 8 | 24
test: A toucan is perched on a branch. How many of its toes point backwards? | Two on each foot | One on each foot | None at all | Three on each foot
test: In The Lion King, Zazu the hornbill gives the king advice. Which part is true to real life? | Red-billed hornbills live on the African savanna | Real hornbills can talk like people | Hornbills live in the Arctic | Hornbills are kept by lions as helpers
test: In a Tarzan film, a kookaburra laughs in an African jungle. What is wrong? | Kookaburras live in Australia, not Africa | Kookaburras never make any sound | Kookaburras live only in the Arctic | Nothing, kookaburras live in every jungle
test: The ibis food runs out and the zoo feeds plain fish. What happens over time? | New feathers grow in paler | The ibis slowly turns bright blue | Nothing changes in its colour at all | The ibis loses all its feathers
test: What would happen to a hummingbird that missed its meals for a whole day? | It could run out of energy, because hovering burns so much | Nothing, as hummingbirds can go for weeks without any food | It would grow bigger | It would start eating seeds
test: kid says the toucan's beak must be super heavy, it's massive lol. right? | No, it is light and full of air spaces | Yes, toucans can hardly lift their heads | Yes, so toucans walk instead of fly | No, it is made of soft rubber
test: The keeper sees a bird has lost weight but it looks fine. What should she think? | It may be ill and hiding it | It is just being lazy | It must be getting ready to fly away | It is perfectly healthy
test: Why would a bird in the wild hide that it is ill? | Weak-looking birds are more likely to be caught | Other birds would steal its nest | It does not want the keeper or its flock to worry about it | Ill birds are always very colourful
test: A pair of birds take turns sitting on their eggs and fetching food. Why does this help? | The chicks survive better with two parents | The eggs must be moved to a new nest every single hour | The parents get bored sitting | It stops the eggs hatching too early
test: Why do many birds nest in spring? | Insects to feed the chicks are most plentiful then | The eggs would overheat in summer | The days are shorter then | Birds only lay eggs at Easter
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the walk-through aviary is furnished with a double set of doors. | To prevent the escape of birds upon the entry of a visitor | To expedite the departure of visitors | To exclude rainfall from the enclosure | To admit two parties of visitors simultaneously through one entrance
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the scarlet ibis? | Its red plumage derives from pigments in the crustaceans it consumes | Its red plumage is produced by exposure to sunlight | It is red solely during the breeding season and white for the remainder of the year | Its plumage is coloured by the mud of its habitat
exam: Ascertain the aggregate number of wings possessed by seven hummingbirds. | Fourteen | Seven | Twenty-one | Twenty-eight
exam: It is given that a captive bird has lost weight while appearing healthy. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Loss of weight is frequently the earliest sign of concealed illness | Loss of weight demonstrates excellent health | Birds lose weight only in the weeks prior to their seasonal migration | Loss of weight is without significance in birds
